    "intent": [
      {
        "id": "objective",
        "key": "objective",
        "order": 0,
        "source": "a4JKLFU7YSug3Wfv",
        "exact": "Build ForgeBoard from scratch in this empty workspace. You own implementation: inspect, plan, create, implement, test, debug and finish using available tools, not merely a description. Work as one persistent Coder, without delegation or other agents.\nForgeBoard is a substantial local-first developer project/task board with a React + TypeScript frontend, Node + TypeScript backend, and npm (not yarn/pnpm). Choose appropriate supporting libraries and architecture. No cloud APIs, telemetry, analytics SaaS, external AI or external cloud database.\nProvide project create/list/edit/delete/open and task create/edit/delete/view within projects. Tasks have title, optional description, status (Backlog, Todo, In Progress, Blocked, Done), priority (Low, Medium, High, Urgent), multiple tags, created/updated timestamps. Support combined project/status/priority/tag filters and free-text search over task titles and descriptions. Initial task page size is 50.\nPersist data locally across backend restart. Ignore runtime databases/user data in Git. Use real Server-Sent Events for task/project mutations and update the frontend without manual refresh, not polling. Backend API port must be 4317. Enforce a maximum task title length of 120 characters in backend validation. Use 750 ms debounce wherever editable/search-like debouncing is used.\nAPI needs input validation, meaningful HTTP status codes, deterministic JSON errors, stable successful responses, missing-resource handling and no client stack traces. Frontend needs loading, error and empty states and keyboard-operable normal controls. Functional clarity matters more than visual polish.\nWrite meaningful automated backend API, validation, persistence, combined filtering/search tests and important frontend behavior tests where practical. Actually execute tests, debug failures and run a real production build. Write a useful README with setup/dev/test/build commands, runtime persistence and architecture. Finish all requirements, preserving unfinished work in your structured plan and reporting verification evidence truthfully.",
        "supersedes": null
      },
